        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "description": "Prepare analytic view query payload to process user request",
            "name": "Prepare Analytics Extract Request",
            "returnType": "object",
            "sourceCode": "const rawOutput = $context.$nodes.PRJ_PJC_BO_FUNCTION_PERFORM_INTELLIGENT_SEARCH?.$output ?? {};\n\nfunction parseJsonCandidate(value) {\n  if (value == null) {\n    return null;\n  }\n  if (typeof value === 'string') {\n    const normalized = value\n      .replace(/^```(?:json)?\\s*/i, '')\n      .replace(/\\s*```$/, '')\n      .trim();\n    if (!normalized) {\n      return null;\n    }\n    return JSON.parse(normalized);\n  }\n  return value;\n}\n\nconst candidates = [\n  rawOutput\n];\n\nfor (const candidate of candidates) {\n  const parsed = parseJsonCandidate(candidate);\n  if (parsed && typeof parsed === 'object' && !Array.isArray(parsed)) {\n    const queries = parsed.queries;\n    if (Array.isArray(queries) && queries.length > 0 && queries[0] && typeof queries[0] === 'object' && !Array.isArray(queries[0])) {\n      return queries[0];\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nthrow new Error('BOSS AI output does not contain a queries array with an object at index 0 for AV extraction.');"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "extract_av_data"
          },
          "code": "PRJ_PJC_CODE_PREPARE_ANALYTICS_EXTRACT_REQUEST",
          "id": "get_av_extract_payload",
          "inputs": [],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"timeout\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"error\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"additionalProperties\": false,\n  \"required\": [\n    \"result\",\n    \"timeout\",\n    \"error\"\n  ]\n}",
          "type": "CODE"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "businessObjectCode": "ORA_PRJ_ORAPRJCOMM_PROJECTCOMMONDATAEXTRACTION",
            "description": "Run analytic view query to process user request",
            "errorNodeId": "1781496759182",
            "functionName": "EXTRACT",
            "name": "Run Analytics Extract Query"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "generate_av_result"
          },
          "code": "PRJ_PJC_BO_FUNCTION_RUN_ANALYTICS_EXTRACT_QUERY",
          "id": "extract_av_data",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "extract_av_data_payload",
              "name": "payload",
              "value": "{{$context.$nodes.PRJ_PJC_CODE_PREPARE_ANALYTICS_EXTRACT_REQUEST.$output.result}}"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{}",
          "type": "BO_FUNCTION"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "description": "Parse analytic view query response and prepare data for final user response",
            "name": "Generate Analytics Response"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "prepare_filter_display_context"
          },
          "code": "PRJ_PJC_LLM_GENERATE_ANALYTICS_RESPONSE",
          "id": "generate_av_result",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "generate_av_result_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "INPUTS\nUser message:\n{{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}\n\nSelected artifact:\n{{$context.$nodes.PRJ_PJC_LLM_IDENTIFY_ARTIFACT.$output.artifact}}\n\nSelected artifact metadata:\n{{$context.$variables.prjPjcVarUqSelectedArtifactMetadata}}\n\nBOSS AI request payload:\n{{$context.$nodes.PRJ_PJC_CODE_PREPARE_INTELLIGENT_SEARCH_REQUEST_PAYLOAD.$output.result}}\n\nBOSS AI AV filter/query payload:\n{{$context.$nodes.PRJ_PJC_CODE_PREPARE_ANALYTICS_EXTRACT_REQUEST.$output.result}}\n\nBOSS extract response:\n{{$context.$nodes.PRJ_PJC_BO_FUNCTION_RUN_ANALYTICS_EXTRACT_QUERY.$output}}\n\nTASK\nGenerate only a compact JSON object that the Redwood Formatter can convert into chat-widget JSON.\n\nOUTPUT SHAPES\nFor text:\n{\n  \"type\": \"text\",\n  \"result\": \"short user-facing message\",\n  \"explanation\": \"concise debug summary covering decision, data source, selected fields, templates, pairing, and amount/currency handling\"\n}\n\nFor table:\n{\n  \"type\": \"table\",\n  \"columns\": [\n    { \"headerText\": \"Column 1\", \"template\": \"\" },\n    { \"headerText\": \"Column 2\", \"template\": \"monetaryTemplate\" }\n  ],\n  \"data\": [\n    [\"row1 col1\", \"{amount:1250,currency:USD}\"],\n    [\"row2 col1\", \"{amount:2500,currency:USD}\"]\n  ],\n  \"explanation\": \"concise debug summary covering decision, data source, column order, templates, pairing, and amount/currency handling\"\n}\n\nDECISION AND DATA RULES\n1. Use BOSS extract response as the source data. The BOSS AI AV filter/query payload explains how aliases such as C0, C1, C2 map to measures and dimensions.\n2. Use text when the user asks for a single summarized value or one answer, such as total, sum, count, how many, average, minimum, maximum, overall, or summary.\n3. Use table when the extract response contains multiple rows, or when the user asks to break down, list, show, display, compare, group, or show values by a dimension.\n4. For text output, form a short natural-language answer using only the mapped values from the extract response. Do not explain how you computed it.\n5. For every table output, include `No.` as the first column object in `columns` with `template` set to an empty string, and populate each row with a 1-based serial number as the first value.\n6. For table output, build user-friendly columns from meaningful measure and dimension alias names in the AV payload after the leading `No.` column. Do not use technical alias names like C0 or C1 as column names.\n7. Include filter dimensions or filter columns implied by the user message when those values are available in extract response or can be mapped from the AV payload.\n8. Do not include hierarchy/internal columns such as level name unless the user specifically asked for hierarchy level information.\n9. Preserve null values as null.\n10. If extract response has no rows or no usable values, return exact text 'No rows were found. '.\n\nAV FIELD LABEL METADATA RULES\n1. The selected artifact metadata is the exact AV measure and dimension label map for the artifact selected by Identify Artifact. It is the primary display-label lookup for AV results.\n2. For every AV table column after `No.`, resolve the underlying measure or dimension name from the BOSS AI AV filter/query payload alias mapping. If the selected artifact metadata contains that exact name, use its mapped label exactly as `headerText`.\n3. For text output, whenever a measure or dimension is mentioned by name, use its mapped label exactly when the selected artifact metadata contains that exact field name.\n4. Apply the lookup only to the selected artifact. Do not use labels from another artifact and do not invent metadata keys.\n5. If the exact measure/dimension name is absent from the selected artifact metadata, follow the existing label, alias, payload, and humanization instructions unchanged.\n\nCOLUMN TEMPLATE RULES\n1. For every table output, return `columns` as an array of objects. Each object must contain `headerText` and `template`.\n2. Return the `columns` array as the only table column-definition structure. The `data` arrays must align positionally with the `columns` array.\n3. Allowed `template` values are exactly `monetaryTemplate`, `dateTemplate`, or an empty string `` for no template.\n4. Decide the template from the selected artifact metadata, query payload aliases, field labels, descriptions, data types, and source value semantics. Do not decide from the display header alone.\n5. Use `monetaryTemplate` for numeric measure columns so they are right aligned in the Redwood table. This includes true money, amount, cost, price, payment, rate, charge, total, value, revenue, expense, budget, balance, quantity, count, percentage, duration, and other columns whose displayed values are only numeric.\n6. For true money, amount, cost, price, payment, rate, charge, total, value, revenue, expense, budget, or balance columns with an associated currency code, the cell value should use Redwood amount schema such as `{amount:1250,currency:USD}`. Numeric non-money measure columns such as `Quantity` or `Count` may keep plain numeric values while still using `monetaryTemplate` for right alignment.\n7. Use `dateTemplate` only for true date or date-time columns. Date-template cell values should be dates or parseable date strings; the formatter will normalize them for Redwood rendering.\n8. Use empty template for `No.`, IDs, identifiers, codes, business numbers that identify records, names, statuses, sources, descriptions, units of measure, and business dimensions. Examples that must use empty template: `No.`, `Project Number`, `Task Number`, `Transaction Number`, `Invoice Number`, `Asset Number`, `Funding Source Number`, `Status`, `Document`, and `Asset Name`.\n9. Do not use `monetaryTemplate` for numeric-looking identifiers or code values, even when every value contains only digits. ID-like and number-like business keys are identifiers, not numeric measures.\n\nNUMBER/NAME PAIRING DISPLAY RULES\n1. When the source response contains both a Number/Code/Identifier value and a Name/Description/Display Name value for the same business attribute, combine them into one table column instead of returning separate columns.\n2. Apply this especially to Project, Task, Contract, Worker, Supplier, Customer, Asset, and similar business reference attributes when both values are available in the same row or mapped query output.\n3. The combined column header must be the accessor label from the selected artifact metadata, not a name/number composite label. Look up the label for the shared accessor or business reference attribute that owns both values. Examples: for `project.name` plus `project.projectNumber`, use the `project` accessor label `Project`; for `task.name` plus `task.taskNumber`, use the `task` accessor label `Project Task`; for `contract.contractName` plus `contract.contractNumber`, use `Contract`.\n4. If an accessor label is not available, use the clearest business reference label without Name, Number, Code, Identifier, Description, or Display Name suffixes.\n5. The combined cell value must be built from the two source values in the same format: `<name value> (<number value>)`. Example: `Store Expansion (PJT-1001)`.\n6. If one side is null, blank, or not present, do not invent it. Use the available value only, and use the accessor/business reference label for the header when available; otherwise use the matching single metadata label.\n7. Do not combine unrelated number/name fields from different accessors or business concepts. Only combine values that metadata, accessor path, query alias meaning, or source object nesting clearly identifies as the same attribute.\n8. The combined column uses empty template ``; do not use `monetaryTemplate` or `dateTemplate` for number/name pairing columns.\n\n\nREDWOOD AMOUNT/CURRENCY VALUE RULES\n1. The LLM is responsible for pairing money fields with currency fields from the same source row/object. Currency may be present in a non-displayed source field; it does not need to be selected as a table column.\n2. When a returned amount, cost, price, payment, rate, charge, total, value, revenue, expense, budget, or balance has an associated currency code anywhere in the same source row/object, return that value in Redwood amount schema string format in the same value position: `{amount:1250,currency:USD}` or `{amount:1250.50,currency:EUR}`.\n3. Numeric non-money measure columns such as quantity, count, percentage, duration, or other number-only measure values should not be converted into Redwood amount schema and should not invent a currency. They may remain plain numbers while their column uses `monetaryTemplate` for right alignment.\n4. Use one display column for the money value. Do not append currency as plain text such as `1250 USD`, do not put amount and currency into separate table columns when they can be paired, and do not mention currency separately for the same value.\n5. Treat fields named or labeled like currency, currencyCode, currencyCd, transactionCurrencyCode, projectCurrencyCode, ledgerCurrencyCode, enteredCurrencyCode, documentCurrencyCode, invoiceCurrencyCode, contractCurrencyCode, or similar as currency-code candidates.\n6. For text output, embed paired money values in the same Redwood amount schema string format, for example `The total burdened cost is {amount:1250,currency:USD}.`\n7. If you cannot safely identify the currency code for a money value, leave the money value as-is and do not invent a currency. Preserve null money values as null.\n\nEXPLANATION DEBUG RULES\n1. Always populate `explanation` with a concise debugging summary, not just the reason for choosing text or table.\n2. For table output, the explanation must briefly cover each major prompt section that affected the result: response format decision, source records used, selected fields and column order, column template choices, number/name pairing, and amount/currency handling.\n3. For text output, briefly cover response format decision, source values used, and why table-only rules such as column templates, pairing, and amount/currency formatting were not applicable or how they were applied.\n4. Keep `explanation` as a single JSON string. Do not include hidden chain-of-thought, step-by-step private reasoning, markdown, comments, or text outside the JSON object.\n\nGENERAL RULES\n1. Return only valid JSON.\n2. Do not return markdown, comments, or text outside the JSON object.\n3. Do not include hidden chain-of-thought in result. Use `explanation` only for the concise debug summary required above."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"type\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"columns\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"headerText\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"template\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"data\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"array\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"explanation\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"type\"\n  ]\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "description": "Parse business view query response and prepare data for final user response",
            "name": "Generate Business View Response"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "prepare_filter_display_context"
          },
          "code": "PRJ_PJC_LLM_GENERATE_BUSINESS_VIEW_RESPONSE",
          "id": "1",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1_prompt",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "INPUTS\nUser message:\n{{$context.$system.$inputMessage}}\n\nSelected artifact:\n{{$context.$nodes.PRJ_PJC_LLM_IDENTIFY_ARTIFACT.$output.artifact}}\n\nSelected artifact metadata, when available:\n{{$context.$variables.prjPjcVarUqSelectedArtifactMetadata}}\n\nBOSS AI response:\n{{$context.$nodes.PRJ_PJC_BO_FUNCTION_PERFORM_INTELLIGENT_SEARCH.$output}}\n\nTASK\nGenerate only a compact JSON object from the BOSS AI response that the next workflow step can convert into Redwood chat-widget JSON.\n\nOUTPUT SHAPES\nFor text:\n{\n  \"type\": \"text\",\n  \"result\": \"short user-facing message\",\n  \"explanation\": \"concise debug summary covering decision, data source, selected fields, templates, pairing, and amount/currency handling\"\n}\n\nFor table:\n{\n  \"type\": \"table\",\n  \"columns\": [\n    { \"headerText\": \"Column 1\", \"template\": \"\" },\n    { \"headerText\": \"Column 2\", \"template\": \"monetaryTemplate\" }\n  ],\n  \"data\": [\n    [\"row1 col1\", \"{amount:1250,currency:USD}\"],\n    [\"row2 col1\", \"{amount:2500,currency:USD}\"]\n  ],\n  \"explanation\": \"concise debug summary covering decision, data source, column order, templates, pairing, and amount/currency handling\"\n}\n\nDECISION AND DATA RULES\n1. Use BOSS AI response as the source data and use response.queries[0].data.items as the primary records when it exists.\n2. Use table when the user asks to list, show, display, compare, break down, summarize multiple rows, or when multiple records are needed for a clear answer.\n3. Strongly use table when the BOSS AI response response.queries[0].data.items have more than one records\n4. Use text when the user asks for one answer, one status, one date, one amount, a yes/no answer, a count, or a concise summary.\n5. Prefer text when there is only one matching record and one or a few fields directly answer the user.\n6. When the response contains no matching records, return exact text 'No rows were found. '.\n\n\n\nCOLUMN TEMPLATE RULES\n1. For every table output, return `columns` as an array of objects. Each object must contain `headerText` and `template`.\n2. Return the `columns` array as the only table column-definition structure. The `data` arrays must align positionally with the `columns` array.\n3. Allowed `template` values are exactly `monetaryTemplate`, `dateTemplate`, or an empty string `` for no template.\n4. Decide the template from the selected artifact metadata, query payload aliases, field labels, descriptions, data types, and source value semantics. Do not decide from the display header alone.\n5. Use `monetaryTemplate` for numeric measure columns so they are right aligned in the Redwood table. This includes true money, amount, cost, price, payment, rate, charge, total, value, revenue, expense, budget, balance, quantity, count, percentage, duration, and other columns whose displayed values are only numeric.\n6. For true money, amount, cost, price, payment, rate, charge, total, value, revenue, expense, budget, or balance columns with an associated currency code, the cell value should use Redwood amount schema such as `{amount:1250,currency:USD}`. Numeric non-money measure columns such as `Quantity` or `Count` may keep plain numeric values while still using `monetaryTemplate` for right alignment.\n7. Use `dateTemplate` only for true date or date-time columns. Date-template cell values should be dates or parseable date strings; the formatter will normalize them for Redwood rendering.\n8. Use empty template for `No.`, IDs, identifiers, codes, business numbers that identify records, names, statuses, sources, descriptions, units of measure, and business dimensions. Examples that must use empty template: `No.`, `Project Number`, `Task Number`, `Transaction Number`, `Invoice Number`, `Asset Number`, `Funding Source Number`, `Status`, `Document`, and `Asset Name`.\n9. Do not use `monetaryTemplate` for numeric-looking identifiers or code values, even when every value contains only digits. ID-like and number-like business keys are identifiers, not numeric measures.\n\n\nNUMBER/NAME PAIRING DISPLAY RULES\n1. When the source response contains both a Number/Code/Identifier value and a Name/Description/Display Name value for the same business attribute, combine them into one table column instead of returning separate columns.\n2. Apply this especially to Project, Task, Contract, Worker, Supplier, Customer, Asset, and similar business reference attributes when both values are available in the same row or mapped query output.\n3. The combined column header must be the accessor label from the selected artifact metadata, not a name/number composite label. Look up the label for the shared accessor or business reference attribute that owns both values. Examples: for `project.name` plus `project.projectNumber`, use the `project` accessor label `Project`; for `task.name` plus `task.taskNumber`, use the `task` accessor label `Project Task`; for `contract.contractName` plus `contract.contractNumber`, use `Contract`.\n4. If an accessor label is not available, use the clearest business reference label without Name, Number, Code, Identifier, Description, or Display Name suffixes.\n5. The combined cell value must be built from the two source values in the same format: `<name value> (<number value>)`. Example: `Store Expansion (PJT-1001)`.\n6. If one side is null, blank, or not present, do not invent it. Use the available value only, and use the accessor/business reference label for the header when available; otherwise use the matching single metadata label.\n7. Do not combine unrelated number/name fields from different accessors or business concepts. Only combine values that metadata, accessor path, query alias meaning, or source object nesting clearly identifies as the same attribute.\n8. The combined column uses empty template ``; do not use `monetaryTemplate` or `dateTemplate` for number/name pairing columns.\n\nREDWOOD AMOUNT/CURRENCY VALUE RULES\n1. The LLM is responsible for pairing money fields with currency fields from the same source row/object. Currency may be present in a non-displayed source field; it does not need to be selected as a table column.\n2. When a returned amount, cost, price, payment, rate, charge, total, value, revenue, expense, budget, or balance has an associated currency code anywhere in the same source row/object, return that value in Redwood amount schema string format in the same value position: `{amount:1250,currency:USD}` or `{amount:1250.50,currency:EUR}`.\n3. Numeric non-money measure columns such as quantity, count, percentage, duration, or other number-only measure values should not be converted into Redwood amount schema and should not invent a currency. They may remain plain numbers while their column uses `monetaryTemplate` for right alignment.\n4. Use one display column for the money value. Do not append currency as plain text such as `1250 USD`, do not put amount and currency into separate table columns when they can be paired, and do not mention currency separately for the same value.\n5. Treat fields named or labeled like currency, currencyCode, currencyCd, transactionCurrencyCode, projectCurrencyCode, ledgerCurrencyCode, enteredCurrencyCode, documentCurrencyCode, invoiceCurrencyCode, contractCurrencyCode, or similar as currency-code candidates.\n6. For text output, embed paired money values in the same Redwood amount schema string format, for example `The total burdened cost is {amount:1250,currency:USD}.`\n7. If you cannot safely identify the currency code for a money value, leave the money value as-is and do not invent a currency. Preserve null money values as null.\n\nTABLE RULES\n1. For every table output, include `No.` as the first column object in `columns` with `template` set to an empty string, and populate each row with a 1-based serial number as the first value.\n2. For each selected artifact below, the listed response field paths define the required table value order. The display column headers do not need to exactly match these field names.\n3. Derive display column headers from Selected artifact metadata when metadata for the field path is available. Use the metadata `label` for the matching field or accessor field path. If metadata is unavailable or no matching label exists, use the fallback header shown below.\n4. If Selected artifact is `projectCostTransactionDetails`, after the leading `No.` column use this display order: [transactionNumber, businessUnit.name, transactionSource.name, document.name, project.name + project.projectNumber, task.name + task.taskNumber, projectCostType.name, projectCostOrganization.name, contract.contractName + contract.contractNumber, fundingSource.fundingSourceNumber, person.names.displayName + person.personDetail.personNumber, transactionDate, quantity, burdenedCostInTransactionCurrency]. Use fallback headers [\"Transaction Number\", \"Business Unit\", \"Cost Transaction Source\", \"Document\", \"Project\", \"Project Task\", \"Cost Type\", \"Cost Organization\", \"Contract\", \"Funding Source\", \"Worker\", \"Transaction Date\", \"Quantity\", \"Burdened Cost in Transaction Currency\"]. For paired entries, use the number/name pairing display rules and output one combined column/value when both fields are available.\n5. If Selected artifact is `projectAssetsView`, after the leading `No.` column use this display order: [assetName, assetType.meaning, project.name + project.projectNumber, actualInServiceOrRetirementDate, totalAssetCost, capitalizedCost, reversalDate, capitalHoldFlag, description, assetBook.name, assetNumber, actualUnits, manufacturerName, modelNumber, serialNumber, tagNumber]. Use fallback headers from the Asset BV metadata: [\"Asset Name\", \"Asset Type\", \"Project\", \"Actual In-Service or Retirement Date\", \"Total Asset Cost\", \"Capitalized Cost\", \"Reversal Date\", \"Capital Hold\", \"Description\", \"Book\", \"Asset Number\", \"Actual Units\", \"Manufacturer\", \"Model Number\", \"Serial Number\", \"Tag Number\"]. For the Project paired entry, use the number/name pairing display rules and output one combined column/value when both fields are available.\n6. If Selected artifact is `projectAssetAssignmentsView`, after the leading `No.` column use this display order: [project.name + project.projectNumber, task.name + task.taskNumber, projectAsset.assetName]. Use fallback headers from the Asset Assignments BV metadata: [\"Project\", \"Project Task\", \"Asset Name\"]. For the Project and Project Task paired entries, use the number/name pairing display rules and output one combined column/value when both fields are available.\n7. If Selected artifact is `unprocessedProjectCostTransactionDetails`, after the leading `No.` column use this display order: [unprocessedTransactionReferenceId, businessUnit.name, transactionSource.name, document.name, batchName, project.name + project.projectNumber, task.name + task.taskNumber, projectCostType.name, projectCostOrganization.name, contract.contractName + contract.contractNumber, fundingSource.fundingSourceNumber, person.names.displayName + person.personDetail.personNumber, transactionDate, quantity, rawCostInTransactionCurrency]. Use fallback headers [\"Unprocessed Transaction Reference\", \"Business Unit\", \"Cost Transaction Source\", \"Document\", \"Project Cost Batch\", \"Project\", \"Project Task\", \"Cost Type\", \"Cost Organization\", \"Contract\", \"Funding Source\", \"Worker\", \"Transaction Date\", \"Quantity\", \"Raw Cost in Transaction Currency\"]. For paired entries, use the number/name pairing display rules and output one combined column/value when both fields are available.\n8. If Selected artifact is `projectAssetLinesView`, after the leading `No.` column use this display order: [id, projectAsset.assetName, project.name + project.projectNumber, projectTask.name + projectTask.taskNumber, description, amount, status.meaning, originalAmount, assetPeriodName, cipCostAccount.concatenatedSegments]. Use fallback headers from the Asset Lines BV metadata: [\"Line Number\", \"Asset Name\", \"Project\", \"Project Task\", \"Description\", \"Amount\", \"Status\", \"Original Amount\", \"Asset Period Name\", \"CIP Cost Account\"]. For the Project and Project Task paired entries, use the number/name pairing display rules and output one combined column/value when both fields are available.\n9. If Selected artifact is `projectContractInvoiceDetails`, use field order [invoiceNumber, invoiceDate, transferStatus, accountingPeriod, project.projectNumber, contract.contractNumber, status.meaning] with fallback headers [\"Invoice Number\", \"Invoice Date\", \"Transfer Status\", \"Accounting Period\", \"Project Number\", \"Contract Number\", \"Status\"].\n10. In `columns`, put `No.` first, then one column object for each field path in the artifact-specific order. In every `data` row, put the 1-based row number first, then values from response.queries[0].data.items in the same field-path order.\n11. If a configured field path is absent in the response, keep that cell as null. Ignore keys that start with \"$\", link metadata, href, etag, and context wrappers unless the user explicitly asked for them.\n12. Preserve null values as null.\n\nEXPLANATION DEBUG RULES\n1. Always populate `explanation` with a concise debugging summary, not just the reason for choosing text or table.\n2. For table output, the explanation must briefly cover each major prompt section that affected the result: response format decision, source records used, selected artifact/table order, column template choices, number/name pairing, and amount/currency handling.\n3. For text output, briefly cover response format decision, source values used, and why table-only rules such as column templates, pairing, and amount/currency formatting were not applicable or how they were applied.\n4. Keep `explanation` as a single JSON string. Do not include hidden chain-of-thought, step-by-step private reasoning, markdown, comments, or text outside the JSON object.\n\nGENERAL RULES\n1. Return only valid JSON.\n2. Do not return markdown, comments, or text outside the JSON object.\n3. Do not include hidden chain-of-thought in result. Use `explanation` only for the concise debug summary required above."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"type\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"columns\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n          \"headerText\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          },\n          \"template\": {\n            \"type\": \"string\"\n          }\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"data\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"array\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"explanation\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"type\"\n  ]\n}",
